t, which led to a five-hour final. " [:a {:href "/athletes/78946"} "Bob Richards"] ", bronze medalist from " [:a {:href "/results/58634"} "1948"] ", was the favorite, having won the 1949-51 AAU, and tying in 1952 with " [:a {:href "/athletes/78684"} "Don Laz"] ", also present in Helsinki. They were the class of the Olympic final, having no misses thru 4.40 (14-5¼), and then were the only two vaulters to clear 4.50 (14-9), both on their second attempts. The competition was thus tied to that point, but Richards made 4.55 (14-11) on his third try to win the gold medal. The best European was Sweden’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/76285"} "Ragnar Lundberg"] ", 1950 European Champion, and he won the bronze medal. He and Soviet " [:a {:href "/athletes/77416"} "Pyotr Denisenko"] " tied at 4.40 (14-5¼), both clearing on the first attempt, and had the same number of misses, but Lundberg had taken one less jump, which was the third tie-breaking rule in this era."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "1025"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78946"} "Bob Richards"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "4.00 (=6)"] [:td {} "4.55 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "1024"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78684"} "Don Laz"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "4.00 (=6)"] [:td {} "4.50 (2)"] [:td {} [